isen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.16 to +0.27), about +2.9\u00b0F since 1887. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.92\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.63 to +1.22) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.72\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.35 to +1.08). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months; January and November fell. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Raleigh's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.17 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 46.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1936 (64.22 in); the driest, 1933 (29.36 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 24 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}